<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Last Supper (Leonardo)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1495–<a href="/years/1498">1498</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Last Supper is a mural painted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonardo-da-vinci">Leonardo da Vinci</a> between about 1495 and 1498 in the refectory of the convent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-maria-delle-grazie-milan">Santa Maria delle Grazie</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, commissioned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludovico-sforza">Ludovico Sforza</a>. It shows the moment Christ announces that one of the apostles will betray him. Because Leonardo used an experimental technique on dry plaster, the paint began to flake within decades, and the work has been restored many times.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/the-last-supper-leonardo.jpg" alt="The Last Supper (Leonardo)" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Leonardo da Vinci · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Last_Supper_-_Leonardo_Da_Vinci_-_High_Resolution_32x16.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The painting measures 460 by 880 centimetres and covers an end wall of the dining hall of the convent. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludovico-sforza">Ludovico Sforza</a> planned to remodel the church as a family mausoleum and commissioned the work to decorate its wall; the lunettes above it carry Sforza coats of arms. On the opposite wall is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crucifixion">Crucifixion</a> fresco by Giovanni Donato da Montorfano, to which Leonardo added figures of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-sforza">Sforza family</a> in tempera. These have deteriorated in much the same way.</p><p>Leonardo did not work continuously, and the start date is uncertain because the convent's archives for the period were destroyed. A document of 1497 indicates that the painting was nearly complete. A prior who complained about the delay was told, according to the report, that if Leonardo could not find the right face for Judas he would use the prior's features.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The scene follows the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospel-of-john">Gospel of John</a>, at the moment after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> announces that one of the apostles will betray him. The twelve react in four groups of three, with Jesus at the centre. Judas, in shadow, clutches a small bag and tips over a salt cellar, and Peter holds a knife. Leonardo seats all the diners on one side of the table, so that none has his back to the viewer, and leaves Judas among them rather than apart. The perspective lines meet at Jesus's turned right cheek.</p><p>The apostles were named from an unsigned mid-sixteenth-century fresco copy; before that only Judas, Peter, John and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> had been identified with certainty. Two early copies attributed to Leonardo's assistants survive, one by Giampietrino in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and one by Cesare da Sesto in Ponte Capriasca, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The damage began early. By 1556 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giorgio-vasari">Giorgio Vasari</a> described the work as a muddle of blots, and in 1652 a doorway was cut through its lower centre and later bricked up. Restorations were attempted by Michelangelo Bellotti in 1726 and Giuseppe Mazza in 1770, and Stefano Barezzi damaged the central section in 1821 when he tried to remove it from the wall. In 1796 French troops used the refectory as an armoury and stable, and on 15 August 1943 Allied bombing struck the room, though sandbags protected the wall.</p><p>From 1978 to 1999 Pinin Brambilla Barcilon led a major restoration, and the painting was returned to display on 28 May 1999 in a sealed, climate-controlled room. Its changes of colour and tone drew criticism, notably from the art historian James Beck. Since Dan Brown's novel The Da Vinci Code (2003), some writers have claimed that the figure beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> is Mary Magdalene, but art historians hold that it is John.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “The Last Supper (Leonardo)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ludovico Sforza’s court employed Leonardo da Vinci from about 1482 to 1499; there he painted the <b>Last Supper</b> in the refectory of Santa Maria delle Grazie, c. 1495–1498, designed a colossal equestrian monument to Francesco Sforza and filled notebooks with engineering and anatomical studies.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Duchy of Milan · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
